= ψ Waveguide =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = A ψ waveguide confines ψ energy to guided modes propagating along a channel axis by imposing Neumann boundary conditions on the transverse walls — modes below the cutoff frequency decay exponentially and cannot propagate, while modes above it travel with group velocity less than c and dispersion determined jointly by the transverse geometry and the effective mass m_eff.
| everyday_analogy   = A garden hose constrains water to flow along its axis rather than spreading in all directions. A microwave waveguide does the same for radio waves — it forces them into specific patterns that travel along the tube's length. A ψ waveguide is the same concept: the TPMS channel walls confine the ψ field laterally, forcing it to propagate only along the channel axis in specific transverse patterns called guided modes. Below a minimum frequency, not even this is possible — the ψ field simply decays rather than propagating.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A cutoff frequency below which no ψ energy can propagate through the channel — determined by both the channel width and the effective mass; (2) A frequency-dependent group velocity that approaches zero near cutoff and approaches c at high frequency; (3) A discrete set of transverse mode patterns, each with its own cutoff frequency; (4) An evanescent coupling mechanism when two waveguide channels are brought close together — the basis of the [[ψ Coupler]] (C5).
| if_you_remember_one_thing = The cutoff frequency is not just a frequency limit — it is a spatial filter. A ψ waveguide of width <math>a</math> transmits ψ at frequencies above <math>\omega_c = \sqrt{m_{\rm eff}^2 + (\pi/a)^2}</math> and blocks it below. By choosing <math>a</math>, the waveguide selects which part of the ψ frequency spectrum can propagate — making it simultaneously a propagation channel and a high-pass filter.
}}

== Mathematical Origin ==

=== Separation of Variables ===

In a rectangular channel of width <math>a</math> (x-direction), height <math>b</math> (y-direction), and infinite length (z-direction), with Neumann conditions on all walls, the Helmholtz equation separates:

:<math>\psi(x,y,z,t) = X(x)\,Y(y)\,e^{i(k_z z - \omega t)}</math>

The transverse equations:

:<math>X''(x) + k_x^2 X(x) = 0, \quad X'(0) = X'(a) = 0 \implies k_x = \frac{m\pi}{a},\; m = 0,1,2,\ldots</math>

:<math>Y''(y) + k_y^2 Y(y) = 0, \quad Y'(0) = Y'(b) = 0 \implies k_y = \frac{n\pi}{b},\; n = 0,1,2,\ldots</math>

The transverse wave number: <math>k_\perp^2 = k_x^2 + k_y^2 = (m\pi/a)^2 + (n\pi/b)^2</math>

The guided wave vector from the dispersion relation:

:<math>\boxed{\omega^2 = k_z^2 + k_\perp^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2}</math>

:<math>k_z = \sqrt{\omega^2 - k_\perp^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2}</math>

For <math>k_z</math> to be real (propagating mode), must have <math>\omega > \omega_c^{(mn)}</math> where:

:<math>\omega_c^{(mn)} = \sqrt{m_{\rm eff}^2 + \left(\frac{m\pi}{a}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{n\pi}{b}\right)^2}</math>

The lowest cutoff (dominant mode, <math>m=1, n=0</math> for <math>a > b</math>):

:<math>\omega_c^{(10)} = \sqrt{m_{\rm eff}^2 + \left(\frac{\pi}{a}\right)^2}</math>

=== Group Velocity ===

:<math>v_g = \frac{d\omega}{dk_z} = \frac{k_z}{\omega} = \frac{\sqrt{\omega^2 - \omega_c^2}}{\omega} < 1</math>

At cutoff: <math>v_g = 0</math> — the wave is stationary, all energy stored in the transverse pattern. Far above cutoff: <math>v_g \to 1</math> — approaches <math>c</math>. This frequency-dependent group velocity means ψ pulses disperse as they propagate — a broadband ψ signal launched into the waveguide arrives at the far end with its frequency components separated in time.

=== Evanescent Decay Below Cutoff ===

For <math>\omega < \omega_c</math>, the axial wave vector is imaginary: <math>k_z = i\kappa</math> where <math>\kappa = \sqrt{\omega_c^2 - \omega^2}</math>. The field decays as:

:<math>\psi(z) \propto e^{-\kappa z}</math>

The evanescent decay length <math>1/\kappa</math> ranges from very short (well below cutoff) to very long (near cutoff). This evanescent field is the mechanism of the [[ψ Coupler]] (C5): two parallel waveguides separated by a small gap exchange ψ energy through their overlapping evanescent tails.

