=== Propagation Loss ===

The propagation loss per unit length (attenuation coefficient):

:<math>\alpha_z = \frac{\omega}{v_g}\,\frac{\tan\delta_\psi}{2} = \frac{\omega^2}{k_z}\,\frac{\tan\delta_\psi}{2}</math>

At 40 kHz, <math>k_z \approx \omega/c</math>, <math>\tan\delta_\psi < 0.005</math>:

:<math>\alpha_z \approx \frac{2\pi \times 4\times10^4}{3\times10^8} \times \frac{0.005}{2} \approx 4.2\times10^{-9}\,\text{m}^{-1} \approx 0.04\,\text{dB/km}</math>

Propagation loss from material absorption is negligible for any practical PsiNet distance. The dominant loss mechanism for PsiNet channels is bending loss at corners and coupling loss at junctions — not material absorption.

=== Bend Loss ===

At a 90° bend with bend radius <math>R</math>, ψ energy couples from the guided mode into radiation modes (ψ leaks out of the guide). The bend loss increases as R decreases. Minimum bend radius for < 1 dB loss:

:<math>R_{\rm min} \approx 3\lambda_g = 3 \times \frac{2\pi}{k_z}</math>

At 40 kHz: <math>\lambda_g \approx 7500</math> m — the ψ guided wavelength is enormous, and the bend radius requirement is impractical for a physical cable. This is the fundamental challenge for PsiNet interconnects: at these frequencies, ψ waveguides must be essentially straight.

The practical implication: PsiNet node connections are point-to-point line-of-sight channels, not routed cables. Free-space ψ propagation (from [[ψ Source]] C11 to [[ψ Detector]] C10) is preferable to guided propagation for inter-node distances > 1 m.

